This is a classic architectural issue known as **Timezone Drift**.
In distributed systems, your database server, your application server (JVM), and your local timezone are often out of sync. If your JVM or Oracle database is running on a cloud server configured to UTC (or a US timezone like PST/EST), it will inherently log the "wrong" date.
For example, when it is 10:00 AM IST on the 23rd, it is still late at night on the 22nd in Western timezones. If your system relies on the server's default clock, it will record yesterday's date.
To fix this permanently across your entire service, we need to explicitly lock the timezone at the application level.
### 1. The JVM Application-Level Fix (Primary Fix)
Your JournalRequest entity uses LocalDateTime.now() to populate the requestDate. By default, this uses the JVM's underlying operating system timezone.
We need to force the Spring Boot application to boot up exclusively in the Asia/Kolkata (IST) timezone.
Open your main application class (e.g., JournalServiceApplication.java) and add a @PostConstruct block:
```java
import jakarta.annotation.PostConstruct;
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import java.util.TimeZone;

@SpringBootApplication
public class JournalServiceApplication {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(JournalServiceApplication.class, args);
    }

    // This forces the entire Spring Boot JVM to run in IST
    // Any call to LocalDateTime.now() will now perfectly match your local date.
    @PostConstruct
    public void init() {
        TimeZone.setDefault(TimeZone.getTimeZone("Asia/Kolkata"));
    }
}

```
### 2. The Bulk Upload / Raw SQL Fix (Secondary Fix)
If you are bypassing JPA for your bulk uploads and using JdbcTemplate to insert rows, you might be letting Oracle use its SYSDATE default for the REQ_DATE column. If the Oracle server is hosted in UTC, SYSDATE will be wrong.
**Instead of letting the database decide the time, pass the JVM's strictly controlled time to the database.**
If you have an INSERT statement in your batchUpdate logic, update it to explicitly set the REQ_DATE using the Java time:
```java
// Inside your bulk insert logic:
String sql = "INSERT INTO JOURNAL_REQUEST (BATCH_ID, REQ_BRANCH_CODE, REQ_CURRENCY, REQ_AMOUNT, REQ_DATE, ...) " +
             "VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ...)";

jdbcTemplate.batchUpdate(sql, new BatchPreparedStatementSetter() {
    @Override
    public void setValues(PreparedStatement ps, int i) throws SQLException {
        // ... set other values ...
        
        // Pass the strictly locked IST time from Java, bypassing Oracle's SYSDATE
        ps.setTimestamp(5, java.sql.Timestamp.valueOf(LocalDateTime.now())); 
    }
    // ...
});

```
### 3. Oracle PL/SQL Procedure Fix
If you look back at the PROCESS_JOURNAL_BATCH procedure we worked on earlier, we used SYSTIMESTAMP and TRUNC(SYSDATE) in several places (like for EXECUTION_DATE and POST_DATE).
If your Oracle DB is returning the 22nd instead of the 23rd, you can explicitly force Oracle to cast the system time to your timezone inside your procedures.
Replace SYSDATE or SYSTIMESTAMP with:
CURRENT_TIMESTAMP AT TIME ZONE 'Asia/Kolkata'
**Example inside your procedure:**
```sql
    -- Instead of SYSTIMESTAMP:
    EXECUTION_DATE = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP AT TIME ZONE 'Asia/Kolkata',

```
Implementing Step 1 will fix all JPA saves (@Entity), and Steps 2/3 will seal the gaps in your high-performance JDBC/PLSQL layers.
